window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["linux"]=[{"id":"linux-f0001","card":"q-linux-001-linux-and-how-it-differs-from-windows","sec":"linux-s002","q":"What is Linux, and how does it differ from Windows?","a":"<p>Linux is an open-source, Unix-like OS that is customizable and free. Windows is proprietary. Linux has open code, no license cost, a hierarchical file system (ext4 vs. NTFS) and a CLI focus (Bash vs. PowerShell).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0002","card":"q-linux-001-linux-and-how-it-differs-from-windows","sec":"linux-s002","q":"Linux kernel vs. distribution?","a":"<p>The kernel is the core OS that manages hardware and processes. A distribution is the kernel plus tools and software (Ubuntu, Fedora). Distros differ in packages and package managers.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0003","card":"q-linux-001-linux-and-how-it-differs-from-windows","sec":"linux-s002","q":"Monolithic vs. microkernel?","a":"<p>Monolithic (Linux): everything runs in the kernel, which is fast. Microkernel (QNX): minimal kernel, modular, slower.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0004","card":"q-linux-001-linux-and-how-it-differs-from-windows","sec":"linux-s002","q":"Why is \"Linux\" technically only the kernel, and why does that matter when you pick a distribution for servers?","a":"<p>Linux is the kernel. The tools, libraries and package manager around it come from the distribution. For servers the choice mostly decides the package format (<code>apt</code>/<code>.deb</code> on Debian and Ubuntu, <code>dnf</code>/<code>.rpm</code> on RHEL and Fedora), the release and support cycle, and the default security stack (SELinux on the RHEL family, AppArmor on Ubuntu). The trade-off is long-term support and vendor backing versus newer packages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0005","card":"q-linux-001-linux-and-how-it-differs-from-windows","sec":"linux-s002","q":"What does it mean that Linux has a monolithic kernel, and how do loadable modules soften the drawbacks?","a":"<p>In a monolithic kernel, drivers, file systems and the network stack run in kernel space, so calls between them are fast function calls. The drawback is that a buggy driver can crash the whole system. Loadable kernel modules let you add or remove drivers at run time without rebooting and keep the base kernel small, but a loaded module still runs with full kernel privileges.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0006","card":"q-linux-001-linux-and-how-it-differs-from-windows","sec":"linux-s002","q":"On Linux, what is different about file paths and case compared with Windows?","a":"<p>Linux has a single tree rooted at <code>/</code> with no drive letters, and other disks are mounted into it. Paths use <code>/</code>, not <code>\\</code>. Names are case-sensitive on typical Linux file systems, so <code>Report.txt</code> and <code>report.txt</code> are two files. Scripts that move from Windows often break on case and on line endings (CRLF versus LF).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0007","card":"q-linux-002-key-components-and-shells","sec":"linux-s003","q":"What are the key Linux components?","a":"<p>Kernel, shell (Bash), file system (ext4), user tools (<code>ls</code>, <code>grep</code>), libraries (<code>glibc</code>), daemons (<code>sshd</code>) and bootloader (GRUB).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0008","card":"q-linux-002-key-components-and-shells","sec":"linux-s003","q":"What is the role of the shell?","a":"<p>The shell (Bash) interprets user commands, talks to the kernel and supports scripting for automation.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0009","card":"q-linux-002-key-components-and-shells","sec":"linux-s003","q":"What are the common Linux shells?","a":"<p>Bash (default, script-friendly), Zsh (advanced completion), Fish (user-friendly), Sh (lightweight), Tcsh (C-like).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0010","card":"q-linux-002-key-components-and-shells","sec":"linux-s003","q":"Why do scripts start with <code>#!/bin/bash</code> and what happens if you leave the line out?","a":"<p>The first line (the shebang) tells the kernel which interpreter runs the file when you execute it directly. Without it, the shell that starts the script tries to run it itself, which can behave differently if that shell is not Bash. Also the file needs the execute bit (<code>chmod +x</code>) to run as <code>./script.sh</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0011","card":"q-linux-002-key-components-and-shells","sec":"linux-s003","q":"Why use <code>#!/bin/sh</code> for a portable script and <code>#!/bin/bash</code> when you use arrays or <code>[[ ]]</code>?","a":"<p><code>sh</code> is the POSIX shell, and on Debian and Ubuntu it is usually <code>dash</code>, which is small and fast but lacks Bash extensions such as arrays, <code>[[ ]]</code> and process substitution. A script using those features under <code>sh</code> fails with syntax errors. Use <code>sh</code> only when you stick to POSIX syntax.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0012","card":"q-linux-002-key-components-and-shells","sec":"linux-s003","q":"What is the difference between a login shell and a non-login interactive shell, and which startup files does Bash read for each?","a":"<p>A login shell (SSH session, <code>su -</code>) reads <code>/etc/profile</code> and then the first found of <code>~/.bash_profile</code>, <code>~/.bash_login</code> or <code>~/.profile</code>. A non-login interactive shell (a new terminal window, or <code>bash</code> typed in a session) reads <code>~/.bashrc</code>. Because of this, many setups source <code>~/.bashrc</code> from the profile file so that settings apply in both cases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0013","card":"q-linux-003-processes-permissions-and-users","sec":"linux-s004","q":"Process vs. thread?","a":"<p>A process is an independent program with its own memory (Firefox). A thread is a lightweight unit inside a process and shares its memory (a browser tab).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0014","card":"q-linux-003-processes-permissions-and-users","sec":"linux-s004","q":"How do Linux file permissions work? Give <code>chmod</code> examples.","a":"<p>Permissions apply to owner, group and others, each with read (4), write (2) and execute (1).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>chmod 755 file   # rwx for owner, rx for group and others\nchmod u+x file   # add execute for owner</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0015","card":"q-linux-003-processes-permissions-and-users","sec":"linux-s004","q":"What are the basic Linux file types?","a":"<p>Regular (<code>-</code>), directory (<code>d</code>), symbolic link (<code>l</code>), hard link, block device (<code>b</code>), character device (<code>c</code>) and named pipe (<code>p</code>). Check with <code>ls -l</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0016","card":"q-linux-003-processes-permissions-and-users","sec":"linux-s004","q":"What is the purpose of the root user?","a":"<p>Root (UID 0) has full system access for admin tasks (<code>sudo apt update</code>). Use it cautiously to avoid damage.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0017","card":"q-linux-003-processes-permissions-and-users","sec":"linux-s004","q":"<code>sudo</code> vs. <code>su</code>?","a":"<p><code>sudo</code> runs a single command as root (<code>sudo cmd</code>). <code>su</code> starts a full root session (<code>su -</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0018","card":"q-linux-003-processes-permissions-and-users","sec":"linux-s004","q":"What do the permission bits mean on a directory, and why can you not enter a directory that has only read permission?","a":"<p>On a directory, read lets you list names, write lets you create, delete or rename entries (given execute), and execute (search) lets you traverse it and reach files inside by name. With read but no execute you can see names, but you cannot open or stat anything inside.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0019","card":"q-linux-003-processes-permissions-and-users","sec":"linux-s004","q":"What does <code>chmod 640 file</code> produce, and what is the common mistake with <code>chmod 777</code>?","a":"<p>640 means owner read and write, group read, others nothing (<code>rw-r-----</code>). <code>chmod 777</code> gives everyone write access, which is a security hole and rarely fixes the real cause of a permission error. Fix ownership with <code>chown</code> or add the right group instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0020","card":"q-linux-003-processes-permissions-and-users","sec":"linux-s004","q":"What do the setuid, setgid and sticky bits do?","a":"<p>Setuid on an executable makes it run with the file owner's privileges (<code>passwd</code> uses it). Setgid on an executable runs it with the group's privileges, and on a directory it makes new files inherit the directory's group. The sticky bit on a directory (<code>/tmp</code>) lets only a file's owner or root delete or rename files in it. Setuid on root-owned programs is a common privilege-escalation target, so audit it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0021","card":"q-linux-003-processes-permissions-and-users","sec":"linux-s004","q":"What does <code>umask 022</code> do to newly created files and directories?","a":"<p><code>umask</code> removes permission bits from the default. Files start at 666 and directories at 777, so with <code>022</code> new files are 644 and new directories are 755. A stricter <code>077</code> leaves new files private to the owner.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0022","card":"q-linux-003-processes-permissions-and-users","sec":"linux-s004","q":"Where does Linux store users, password hashes and groups, and why are the hashes not in the file everyone can read?","a":"<p>Users are in <code>/etc/passwd</code>, which is world-readable because many programs map UIDs to names. Hashes are in <code>/etc/shadow</code>, readable only by root, so attackers cannot copy them for offline cracking. Groups are in <code>/etc/group</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0023","card":"q-linux-003-processes-permissions-and-users","sec":"linux-s004","q":"Why should you edit sudo rules with <code>visudo</code> instead of opening <code>/etc/sudoers</code> in a normal editor?","a":"<p><code>visudo</code> locks the file and checks the syntax before saving. A syntax error in <code>/etc/sudoers</code> can lock every user out of <code>sudo</code>, so the check prevents that.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0024","card":"q-linux-003-processes-permissions-and-users","sec":"linux-s004","q":"Why does <code>sudo echo hi &gt; /root/file</code> fail with permission denied even though <code>echo</code> runs as root?","a":"<p>The redirection <code>&gt;</code> is done by your own shell before <code>sudo</code> starts, so the file is opened with your privileges. Use <code>echo hi | sudo tee /root/file</code> or <code>sudo sh -c 'echo hi &gt; /root/file'</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0025","card":"q-linux-004-boot-process","sec":"linux-s005","q":"What is the Linux boot process?","a":"<p>BIOS/UEFI, then GRUB loads the kernel and initramfs, then the kernel initializes hardware and mounts root, then systemd starts services, then the login prompt appears.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0026","card":"q-linux-004-boot-process","sec":"linux-s005","q":"How do you troubleshoot a boot failure?","a":"<p>Check GRUB (press <code>e</code> to edit, boot to single-user mode), read logs with <code>journalctl -b</code>, or use a live CD to run <code>fsck</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0027","card":"q-linux-004-boot-process","sec":"linux-s005","q":"What is the role of <code>initramfs</code> during boot, and what breaks if it is missing a driver?","a":"<p><code>initramfs</code> is a small temporary root file system loaded into memory with the kernel. It holds the drivers and tools needed to find and mount the real root file system (for example RAID, LVM, encryption or storage controller drivers). If the driver for the root disk is missing, the boot stops with an error such as \"cannot mount root\" or drops to an emergency shell.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0028","card":"q-linux-004-boot-process","sec":"linux-s005","q":"What is the difference between BIOS with MBR and UEFI with GPT?","a":"<p>BIOS reads a boot loader from the first sector (MBR) of the disk, and MBR partitioning supports disks up to 2 TB and four primary partitions. UEFI reads boot loaders from an EFI System Partition and works with GPT, which supports large disks and many partitions. UEFI also supports Secure Boot.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0029","card":"q-linux-004-boot-process","sec":"linux-s005","q":"Where do you change kernel parameters for one boot in GRUB, and where do you make the change permanent?","a":"<p>For one boot, press <code>e</code> on the GRUB entry, edit the <code>linux</code> line, and boot. To make it permanent, edit <code>GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX</code> in <code>/etc/default/grub</code> and regenerate the config (<code>update-grub</code> on Debian and Ubuntu, <code>grub2-mkconfig -o ...</code> on RHEL family). Editing the final config file directly is overwritten on the next kernel update.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0030","card":"q-linux-004-boot-process","sec":"linux-s005","q":"A server hangs at boot after an <code>/etc/fstab</code> edit. What is the likely cause and how do you recover?","a":"<p>A wrong UUID, device name or mount option makes a required mount fail, and systemd waits and then drops to emergency mode. Log in to the emergency shell with the root password, remount root read-write (<code>mount -o remount,rw /</code>), fix <code>/etc/fstab</code>, and reboot. Adding <code>nofail</code> to non-critical mounts prevents this kind of hang.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0031","card":"q-linux-005-file-system-hierarchy-and-disk-space","sec":"linux-s006","q":"What is the Linux file system hierarchy?","a":"<p>Root (<code>/</code>) holds <code>/etc</code> (configs), <code>/var</code> (logs), <code>/home</code> (users), <code>/bin</code> (binaries), <code>/usr</code> (programs), <code>/tmp</code> (temp) and <code>/proc</code> (kernel info).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0032","card":"q-linux-005-file-system-hierarchy-and-disk-space","sec":"linux-s006","q":"How do you check disk space?","a":"<p><code>df -h</code> shows file system usage. <code>du -sh /dir</code> shows directory size.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0033","card":"q-linux-005-file-system-hierarchy-and-disk-space","sec":"linux-s006","q":"What does <code>lsblk</code> do?","a":"<p>It lists block devices (disks and partitions) with names, mount points and sizes (<code>sda1</code> at <code>/</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0034","card":"q-linux-005-file-system-hierarchy-and-disk-space","sec":"linux-s006","q":"What is <code>/proc</code>?","a":"<p>A virtual file system for kernel and process info (<code>/proc/cpuinfo</code>, <code>/proc/[PID]</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0035","card":"q-linux-005-file-system-hierarchy-and-disk-space","sec":"linux-s006","q":"What is the difference between <code>/bin</code>, <code>/sbin</code>, <code>/usr/bin</code> and <code>/usr/local/bin</code>?","a":"<p><code>/bin</code> and <code>/sbin</code> hold essential commands (user and admin), and <code>/usr/bin</code> and <code>/usr/sbin</code> hold the bulk of installed programs. On modern distributions <code>/bin</code> is often a symlink to <code>/usr/bin</code> (the merged-usr layout). <code>/usr/local/bin</code> is for software you install yourself, so package upgrades do not touch it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0036","card":"q-linux-005-file-system-hierarchy-and-disk-space","sec":"linux-s006","q":"Why does <code>df -h</code> show 100% used while <code>du</code> on the file system adds up to much less?","a":"<p>A process may still hold a deleted file open. The space is not freed until the process closes it. Find these with <code>lsof +L1</code> or <code>lsof | grep deleted</code>, then restart or signal the process. Another cause is a mount hiding files under a mount point, and reserved blocks on ext4 also count as used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0037","card":"q-linux-005-file-system-hierarchy-and-disk-space","sec":"linux-s006","q":"What is an inode, and what does it store?","a":"<p>An inode is a file system record holding a file's metadata: type, permissions, owner, size, timestamps and where its data blocks are. It does not hold the file name, which lives in the directory entry. Each file uses one inode.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0038","card":"q-linux-005-file-system-hierarchy-and-disk-space","sec":"linux-s006","q":"What does <code>du -xh --max-depth=1 /var | sort -h</code> do, and why is <code>-x</code> used?","a":"<p>It prints the size of each first-level directory under <code>/var</code>, sorted from smallest to largest, so the biggest consumer is the last line. <code>-h</code> prints human-readable sizes, which <code>sort -h</code> understands. <code>--max-depth=1</code> limits the output to one level. <code>-x</code> keeps <code>du</code> on a single file system, so it does not descend into other mounts (for example a separate <code>/var/lib/docker</code> volume or <code>/proc</code>) and skew the totals or run slowly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0039","card":"q-linux-005-file-system-hierarchy-and-disk-space","sec":"linux-s006","q":"Why mount <code>/var</code> or <code>/home</code> on a separate partition?","a":"<p>A runaway log or a user's files then cannot fill the root file system and crash the system. Separate mounts also allow different options (such as <code>noexec</code> or <code>nosuid</code> on <code>/tmp</code>) and independent quotas. The cost is less flexible space allocation, which LVM helps with.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0040","card":"q-linux-006-links-searching-and-archives","sec":"linux-s007","q":"Soft vs. hard links?","a":"<p>A soft link (<code>ln -s</code>) is a path reference. It breaks if the target is deleted and can cross file systems. A hard link (<code>ln</code>) shares the same inode, persists if the target is deleted and must stay on the same file system.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0041","card":"q-linux-006-links-searching-and-archives","sec":"linux-s007","q":"How do you find files with <code>find</code> and <code>locate</code>?","a":"<p><code>find /home -name \"file.txt\"</code> searches in real time. <code>locate file.txt</code> is faster because it uses a database.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0042","card":"q-linux-006-links-searching-and-archives","sec":"linux-s007","q":"How do you compress and decompress files?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tar -czvf file.tar.gz dir     # compress\nzip file.zip file\ntar -xzvf file.tar.gz         # decompress\nunzip file.zip</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0043","card":"q-linux-006-links-searching-and-archives","sec":"linux-s007","q":"Why can a hard link not point to a directory or to a file on another file system?","a":"<p>A hard link is another directory entry for the same inode number, and inode numbers are only unique inside one file system. Directory hard links are blocked because they could create loops in the tree. A symbolic link stores a path, so it can cross file systems and point to directories.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0044","card":"q-linux-006-links-searching-and-archives","sec":"linux-s007","q":"How do you find out how many hard links a file has and whether two names are the same file?","a":"<p><code>ls -l</code> shows the link count in the second column, and <code>ls -i</code> shows the inode number. Two names with the same inode on the same file system are the same file. The data is freed only when the count drops to zero and no process has it open.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0045","card":"q-linux-006-links-searching-and-archives","sec":"linux-s007","q":"What is the difference between <code>tar -czvf</code> and <code>tar -cjvf</code>, and what does each flag in <code>-czvf</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>c</code> creates an archive, <code>z</code> compresses with gzip, <code>v</code> lists files verbosely, and <code>f</code> names the archive file, so <code>f</code> must come last before the file name. <code>j</code> uses bzip2 instead, which compresses smaller and runs slower. <code>x</code> extracts, and <code>t</code> lists contents without extracting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0046","card":"q-linux-006-links-searching-and-archives","sec":"linux-s007","q":"In <code>find</code>, what does <code>find /var/log -name \"*.log\" -mtime +7 -exec rm {} \\;</code> do, and what is the safer way to try it?","a":"<p>It finds files ending in <code>.log</code> under <code>/var/log</code> that were last modified more than 7 days ago and runs <code>rm</code> on each (<code>{}</code> is the found file). Run it first without <code>-exec</code>, or with <code>-print</code>, to check the matches. <code>-exec ... {} +</code> batches many files into one command and is faster than <code>\\;</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0047","card":"q-linux-006-links-searching-and-archives","sec":"linux-s007","q":"Why might <code>locate</code> not find a file you just created?","a":"<p><code>locate</code> reads a prebuilt database (updated by <code>updatedb</code>, usually once a day), so new files are missing until the next update. Run <code>sudo updatedb</code>, or use <code>find</code> for current results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0048","card":"q-linux-007-viewing-files-redirection-and-pipes","sec":"linux-s008","q":"<code>cat</code> vs. <code>more</code> vs. <code>less</code>?","a":"<p><code>cat</code> dumps the full file. <code>more</code> pages down only. <code>less</code> scrolls both ways and is searchable.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0049","card":"q-linux-007-viewing-files-redirection-and-pipes","sec":"linux-s008","q":"How do you redirect output? Explain <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;</code> and <code>2&gt;</code>.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>echo hi &gt; file      # overwrite\necho hi &gt;&gt; file     # append\nls bad 2&gt; err.log   # redirect errors</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0050","card":"q-linux-007-viewing-files-redirection-and-pipes","sec":"linux-s008","q":"What is piping?","a":"<p>A pipe (<code>|</code>) sends one command's output to another's input, as in <code>ls | grep txt</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0051","card":"q-linux-007-viewing-files-redirection-and-pipes","sec":"linux-s008","q":"What does <code>2&gt;&amp;1</code> do, and why does the order matter in <code>cmd &gt; out.log 2&gt;&amp;1</code>?","a":"<p><code>2&gt;&amp;1</code> points standard error at wherever standard output currently points. Redirections are processed left to right, so <code>&gt; out.log</code> first sends stdout to the file, and then <code>2&gt;&amp;1</code> sends stderr to the same file. Written as <code>2&gt;&amp;1 &gt; out.log</code>, stderr goes to the terminal and only stdout goes to the file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0052","card":"q-linux-007-viewing-files-redirection-and-pipes","sec":"linux-s008","q":"What are file descriptors 0, 1 and 2?","a":"<p>0 is standard input, 1 is standard output and 2 is standard error. Redirection operators act on them, for example <code>&lt;</code> on 0, <code>&gt;</code> on 1 and <code>2&gt;</code> on 2.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0053","card":"q-linux-007-viewing-files-redirection-and-pipes","sec":"linux-s008","q":"What does <code>tee</code> do in a pipeline, and when do you need <code>tee -a</code>?","a":"<p><code>tee</code> copies its input to a file and also passes it on to standard output, so you can save and watch at the same time. By default it overwrites the file, and <code>-a</code> appends. It is also the usual way to write to a root-owned file with <code>sudo</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0054","card":"q-linux-007-viewing-files-redirection-and-pipes","sec":"linux-s008","q":"How do you watch a log file as it grows, and what is the difference between <code>tail -f</code> and <code>tail -F</code>?","a":"<p><code>tail -f /var/log/app.log</code> follows new lines. If log rotation replaces the file, <code>-f</code> keeps following the old file descriptor and goes quiet, whereas <code>-F</code> re-opens the file by name and keeps following.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0055","card":"q-linux-007-viewing-files-redirection-and-pipes","sec":"linux-s008","q":"What do <code>grep -r</code>, <code>-i</code>, <code>-v</code> and <code>-c</code> do?","a":"<p><code>-r</code> searches directories recursively, <code>-i</code> ignores case, <code>-v</code> inverts the match to show non-matching lines, and <code>-c</code> prints only the count of matching lines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0056","card":"q-linux-007-viewing-files-redirection-and-pipes","sec":"linux-s008","q":"What does <code>/dev/null</code> do and how is it used in a command?","a":"<p>It discards everything written to it and returns end-of-file when read. Use <code>cmd &gt; /dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1</code> to silence all output of a command, or <code>2&gt;/dev/null</code> to hide only errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0057","card":"q-linux-008-listing-and-killing-processes","sec":"linux-s009","q":"How do you list running processes?","a":"<p><code>ps aux</code> (all processes), <code>top</code> (real time), <code>htop</code> (user-friendly).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0058","card":"q-linux-008-listing-and-killing-processes","sec":"linux-s009","q":"Difference between <code>kill</code>, <code>pkill</code> and <code>killall</code>?","a":"<p><code>kill -9 PID</code> works by PID. <code>pkill -9 name</code> matches a pattern. <code>killall name</code> matches the exact name.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0059","card":"q-linux-008-listing-and-killing-processes","sec":"linux-s009","q":"How do you run a process in the background? What does <code>&amp;</code> do?","a":"<p>Append <code>&amp;</code> (<code>sleep 100 &amp;</code>) to run it in the background and free the terminal. Use <code>jobs</code> and <code>fg</code> to manage it.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0060","card":"q-linux-008-listing-and-killing-processes","sec":"linux-s009","q":"What is a zombie process and how do you identify and remove it?","a":"<p>A zombie is a terminated process that its parent has not reaped. Find it with <code>ps aux | grep Z</code>. Remove it by killing the parent (<code>kill -9 parent_PID</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0061","card":"q-linux-008-listing-and-killing-processes","sec":"linux-s009","q":"Why try <code>kill PID</code> (SIGTERM) before <code>kill -9 PID</code> (SIGKILL)?","a":"<p>SIGTERM (15) can be caught, so the process can flush buffers, close files and remove its pid file. SIGKILL (9) cannot be caught or ignored, so the kernel ends the process immediately and no cleanup runs, which can leave corrupt data or stale lock files. Use <code>-9</code> only when SIGTERM fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0062","card":"q-linux-008-listing-and-killing-processes","sec":"linux-s009","q":"What does SIGHUP commonly do for daemons, and what does <code>kill -l</code> show?","a":"<p>Many daemons re-read their configuration on SIGHUP (<code>kill -HUP PID</code>) without restarting. <code>kill -l</code> lists all signal names and numbers. SIGSTOP (cannot be caught) pauses a process and SIGCONT resumes it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0063","card":"q-linux-008-listing-and-killing-processes","sec":"linux-s009","q":"Why can a process stay in the state <code>D</code> in <code>ps</code> and why does <code>kill -9</code> not remove it?","a":"<p>State <code>D</code> is uninterruptible sleep, usually waiting on disk or network I/O such as a hung NFS mount or a failing disk. Signals are only delivered when the call returns, so even SIGKILL waits. Fix the underlying I/O problem, since a reboot is often the last resort.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0064","card":"q-linux-008-listing-and-killing-processes","sec":"linux-s009","q":"What do the <code>ps aux</code> columns STAT, VSZ and RSS mean?","a":"<p>STAT is the process state (<code>R</code> running, <code>S</code> sleeping, <code>D</code> uninterruptible, <code>T</code> stopped, <code>Z</code> zombie). VSZ is virtual memory size, including mapped but unused memory. RSS is the resident set, the physical RAM the process holds now. RSS is the better indicator of real memory use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0065","card":"q-linux-008-listing-and-killing-processes","sec":"linux-s009","q":"Why does a background job die when you close your SSH session and how do you prevent it?","a":"<p>When the terminal closes, the shell sends SIGHUP to its jobs. Start the job with <code>nohup cmd &amp;</code> so it ignores SIGHUP, or run it in <code>tmux</code> or <code>screen</code>, or better, make it a systemd service.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0066","card":"q-linux-008-listing-and-killing-processes","sec":"linux-s009","q":"Why is a zombie process harmless to memory but still a problem in large numbers?","a":"<p>A zombie has already exited and holds only an entry in the process table with its exit status. It uses no CPU or memory, but it occupies a PID, and a flood can exhaust the PID limit. Killing a zombie does nothing because it is already dead. The parent must call <code>wait()</code>, or the parent must exit so <code>init</code> adopts and reaps the zombie.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0067","card":"q-linux-009-cpu-memory-and-priority","sec":"linux-s010","q":"How do you check CPU and memory usage?","a":"<p><code>top</code> (live CPU and memory), <code>free -h</code> (memory snapshot), <code>vmstat 1</code> (detailed stats).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0068","card":"q-linux-009-cpu-memory-and-priority","sec":"linux-s010","q":"What are <code>nice</code> and <code>renice</code> for?","a":"<p><code>nice -n 10 cmd</code> sets priority at launch (-20 high to 19 low). <code>renice 15 -p PID</code> adjusts a running process.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0069","card":"q-linux-009-cpu-memory-and-priority","sec":"linux-s010","q":"How does Linux manage memory?","a":"<p>Virtual memory maps addresses and paging swaps to disk. <code>free -h</code> shows swap.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0070","card":"q-linux-009-cpu-memory-and-priority","sec":"linux-s010","q":"In <code>top</code>, what do <code>us</code>, <code>sy</code>, <code>wa</code> and <code>st</code> in the CPU line mean?","a":"<p><code>us</code> is time in user programs, <code>sy</code> is time in the kernel, <code>wa</code> is time waiting for I/O, and <code>st</code> is time stolen by the hypervisor on a virtual machine. High <code>wa</code> points at slow disks, and high <code>st</code> means a noisy neighbour or an oversubscribed host.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0071","card":"q-linux-009-cpu-memory-and-priority","sec":"linux-s010","q":"What does the load average mean, and how do you judge a value of 8 on a server?","a":"<p>Load average is the average number of tasks that are running or waiting to run (and on Linux also in uninterruptible I/O wait) over 1, 5 and 15 minutes. Compare it to the CPU count (<code>nproc</code>). A load of 8 is fine on 16 cores and overloaded on 2. The three numbers show if load is rising or falling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0072","card":"q-linux-009-cpu-memory-and-priority","sec":"linux-s010","q":"How do you read the output of <code>free -h</code>, and why is a low \"free\" value not a problem?","a":"<p>Linux uses spare RAM as the page cache for files, so \"free\" is often small. The <code>available</code> column estimates how much memory programs can still use, including cache that can be released. Worry when <code>available</code> is low and swap is in heavy use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0073","card":"q-linux-009-cpu-memory-and-priority","sec":"linux-s010","q":"What does the OOM killer do and how do you see that it ran?","a":"<p>When memory and swap run out, the kernel kills a process, chosen by an <code>oom_score</code> that favors large memory users, to keep the system alive. Look for \"Out of memory: Killed process\" with <code>dmesg -T | grep -i oom</code> or <code>journalctl -k</code>. You can lower a process's likelihood through <code>oom_score_adj</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0074","card":"q-linux-009-cpu-memory-and-priority","sec":"linux-s010","q":"What does a positive and a negative nice value do, and who may set negative values?","a":"<p>Nice runs from -20 (highest priority) to 19 (lowest), and a higher value means the process is nicer to others and gets less CPU when there is contention. Only root can lower the value to less than the current one or set a negative one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0075","card":"q-linux-009-cpu-memory-and-priority","sec":"linux-s010","q":"What does high swap usage with high <code>si</code> and <code>so</code> in <code>vmstat</code> tell you?","a":"<p>Heavy swapping in and out (thrashing) means the working set does not fit in RAM, and everything slows because disk is far slower than memory. Add RAM, reduce memory use, or limit the workload. Adding more swap only hides the problem.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0076","card":"q-linux-010-fork-exec-and-daemons","sec":"linux-s011","q":"<code>fork()</code> vs. <code>exec()</code>?","a":"<p><code>fork()</code> clones the process. <code>exec()</code> replaces the process with a new program. They are often used together.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0077","card":"q-linux-010-fork-exec-and-daemons","sec":"linux-s011","q":"What is a daemon and how is one created?","a":"<p>A daemon is a background service (<code>sshd</code>). To create one: fork, let the parent exit, call <code>setsid()</code>, redirect I/O and run a loop.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0078","card":"q-linux-010-fork-exec-and-daemons","sec":"linux-s011","q":"Why does a shell use <code>fork()</code> and then <code>exec()</code> to run a command, rather than <code>exec()</code> directly?","a":"<p><code>exec()</code> replaces the current process, so calling it in the shell would end the shell. The shell forks a child, and the child calls <code>exec()</code> to become the new program while the parent shell waits and keeps running. Using the two calls separately also lets the child set up redirections and pipes between the two steps.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0079","card":"q-linux-010-fork-exec-and-daemons","sec":"linux-s011","q":"What is an orphan process and who becomes its parent?","a":"<p>An orphan is a running process whose parent has exited. The kernel re-parents it to PID 1 (<code>init</code> or <code>systemd</code>, or a sub-reaper), which reaps it when it ends.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0080","card":"q-linux-010-fork-exec-and-daemons","sec":"linux-s011","q":"Why is running a service under systemd better than a hand-made daemon with <code>setsid</code>?","a":"<p>systemd handles the daemonizing, restarts the service on failure (<code>Restart=on-failure</code>), orders it against other units, captures stdout and stderr into the journal, and can apply resource limits and sandboxing. A hand-made daemon has to do all of that itself. Run the process in the foreground and let systemd manage it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0081","card":"q-linux-011-scheduling-and-services","sec":"linux-s012","q":"How do you schedule tasks?","a":"<p><code>cron</code> is for recurring jobs (<code>crontab -e</code>, for example <code>0 2 * * * script</code>). <code>at</code> is for one-time jobs (<code>at 10pm -f script</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0082","card":"q-linux-011-scheduling-and-services","sec":"linux-s012","q":"What is <code>systemctl</code> for?","a":"<p>It manages systemd services (<code>systemctl start sshd</code>, <code>enable</code>, <code>status</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0083","card":"q-linux-011-scheduling-and-services","sec":"linux-s012","q":"What are the five time fields in a crontab line, and what does <code>*/15 * * * *</code> mean?","a":"<p>In order: minute, hour, day of month, month and day of week. <code>*/15 * * * *</code> runs every 15 minutes. <code>0 2 * * 1</code> runs at 02:00 every Monday.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0084","card":"q-linux-011-scheduling-and-services","sec":"linux-s012","q":"Why does a cron job work in your shell but fail when cron runs it?","a":"<p>Cron runs with a minimal environment: a short <code>PATH</code>, no profile files and a different working directory. Use absolute paths to commands, set <code>PATH</code> or needed variables at the top of the crontab, and redirect output (<code>&gt;&gt; /var/log/job.log 2&gt;&amp;1</code>) so you can see errors. Also remember that <code>%</code> has a special meaning in crontab lines and must be escaped.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0085","card":"q-linux-011-scheduling-and-services","sec":"linux-s012","q":"How does a systemd timer differ from cron?","a":"<p>A timer unit triggers a service unit on a calendar (<code>OnCalendar=</code>) or after a delay from boot (<code>OnBootSec=</code>). Compared with cron, it logs to the journal, can run missed jobs after downtime (<code>Persistent=true</code>), can use dependencies and resource limits, and you inspect it with <code>systemctl list-timers</code>. It takes two unit files, which is more setup than one crontab line.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0086","card":"q-linux-011-scheduling-and-services","sec":"linux-s012","q":"What is the difference between <code>systemctl enable</code> and <code>systemctl start</code>?","a":"<p><code>start</code> runs the service now and does not survive a reboot. <code>enable</code> creates the links so the service starts at boot but does not start it now. Use <code>enable --now</code> to do both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0087","card":"q-linux-011-scheduling-and-services","sec":"linux-s012","q":"Where do you put your own systemd unit file and what must you run after editing it?","a":"<p>Put your own units in <code>/etc/systemd/system/</code>, because <code>/usr/lib/systemd/system/</code> belongs to packages and is overwritten on updates. After creating or changing a unit, run <code>systemctl daemon-reload</code> so systemd re-reads it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0088","card":"q-linux-011-scheduling-and-services","sec":"linux-s012","q":"What does <code>Restart=on-failure</code> in a <code>[Service]</code> section do, and how do you stop a crash loop from restarting forever?","a":"<p>It restarts the service when the process exits with a non-zero code or is killed by a signal, but not after a clean stop. systemd limits restart bursts with <code>StartLimitIntervalSec</code> and <code>StartLimitBurst</code> (in the <code>[Unit]</code> and <code>[Service]</code> sections depending on version), after which the unit enters a failed state. Use <code>RestartSec=</code> to add a delay.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0089","card":"q-linux-012-network-configuration-and-ports","sec":"linux-s013","q":"How do you check network config?","a":"<p><code>ip addr</code> (modern, shows IPs) or <code>ifconfig</code> (older, IPs and MAC).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0090","card":"q-linux-012-network-configuration-and-ports","sec":"linux-s013","q":"What are <code>netstat</code> and <code>ss</code> for?","a":"<p><code>netstat -tuln</code> lists ports and connections. <code>ss -tuln</code> is the faster, modern alternative.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0091","card":"q-linux-012-network-configuration-and-ports","sec":"linux-s013","q":"How do you set a static IP?","a":"<p>Edit <code>/etc/netplan</code> on Ubuntu (<code>addresses: [192.168.1.100/24]</code>) or <code>/etc/sysconfig/network-scripts</code> on CentOS, then apply with <code>netplan apply</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0092","card":"q-linux-012-network-configuration-and-ports","sec":"linux-s013","q":"What is <code>iptables</code>?","a":"<p>It configures firewall rules. This one allows SSH:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0093","card":"q-linux-012-network-configuration-and-ports","sec":"linux-s013","q":"What does each flag in <code>ss -tulnp</code> do, and why does <code>-p</code> need root to show every process?","a":"<p><code>-t</code> shows TCP sockets, <code>-u</code> UDP sockets, <code>-l</code> listening sockets only, <code>-n</code> numeric ports and addresses with no name lookups, and <code>-p</code> the owning process. Without root, <code>-p</code> shows only your own processes, because other users' process information is protected.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0094","card":"q-linux-012-network-configuration-and-ports","sec":"linux-s013","q":"What do the <code>ss</code> states LISTEN, ESTABLISHED and TIME_WAIT mean?","a":"<p>LISTEN means a server socket is waiting for connections. ESTABLISHED is an active connection. TIME_WAIT is the side that closed first waiting a short time so late packets are not mistaken for a new connection. Many TIME_WAIT sockets are normal on busy clients.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0095","card":"q-linux-012-network-configuration-and-ports","sec":"linux-s013","q":"Why does a service bound to <code>127.0.0.1:5432</code> accept local connections only, and what changes with <code>0.0.0.0</code>?","a":"<p><code>127.0.0.1</code> is the loopback interface and is unreachable from other hosts. <code>0.0.0.0</code> binds to all IPv4 addresses, so remote hosts can connect if the firewall allows it. Binding to loopback is a safe default for internal services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0096","card":"q-linux-012-network-configuration-and-ports","sec":"linux-s013","q":"Why does <code>netplan apply</code> over SSH carry a risk, and what is the safer command?","a":"<p>A wrong static IP or gateway can drop your SSH session and leave you without remote access. <code>netplan try</code> applies the change and rolls it back automatically if you do not confirm within a timeout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0097","card":"q-linux-012-network-configuration-and-ports","sec":"linux-s013","q":"How do you troubleshoot \"I cannot reach a remote host\" step by step on Linux?","a":"<p>Check the local interface and address (<code>ip addr</code>), the default route (<code>ip route</code>), the gateway (<code>ping</code>), then name resolution (<code>dig</code> or <code>getent hosts name</code>), then the path (<code>traceroute</code>) and finally the port (<code>nc -vz host port</code> or <code>curl -v</code>). Each step narrows the layer: link, routing, DNS, network path, then firewall or application.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0098","card":"q-linux-012-network-configuration-and-ports","sec":"linux-s013","q":"In iptables, why does the order of rules matter, and what does <code>-A</code> versus <code>-I</code> do?","a":"<p>Rules in a chain are checked top to bottom and the first match decides. <code>-A</code> appends to the end, so a broad DROP earlier in the chain can hide your ACCEPT. <code>-I</code> inserts at the top (or a given position). Rules added this way are not saved across reboots unless you save them with a tool such as <code>iptables-save</code> or <code>netfilter-persistent</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0099","card":"q-linux-012-network-configuration-and-ports","sec":"linux-s013","q":"What is the difference between <code>iptables</code>, <code>nftables</code> and <code>ufw</code>?","a":"<p><code>iptables</code> and <code>nftables</code> are interfaces to the kernel's netfilter. <code>nftables</code> is the newer framework that replaces <code>iptables</code> on modern distributions. <code>ufw</code> is a simple front end for Ubuntu that writes the rules for you. Use <code>ufw</code> for simple host rules and <code>nftables</code> or <code>iptables</code> for complex ones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0100","card":"q-linux-013-ssh","sec":"linux-s014","q":"What is SSH and how is it used?","a":"<p>SSH is secure remote access, as in <code>ssh john@192.168.1.10</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0101","card":"q-linux-013-ssh","sec":"linux-s014","q":"How do you generate and configure SSH keys?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ssh-keygen -t rsa\nssh-copy-id user@host\nchmod 700 ~/.ssh</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0102","card":"q-linux-013-ssh","sec":"linux-s014","q":"Why is key-based SSH authentication preferred to passwords, and which file on the server holds the allowed public keys?","a":"<p>Keys cannot be guessed by brute force like passwords, and the private key never travels over the network. The server stores allowed public keys in <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code> of the target user. The private key stays on the client, ideally protected with a passphrase and an agent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0103","card":"q-linux-013-ssh","sec":"linux-s014","q":"SSH key login still asks for a password. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check permissions: <code>~/.ssh</code> should be <code>700</code> and <code>authorized_keys</code> <code>600</code>, and the home directory must not be writable by group or others, since <code>sshd</code> ignores keys otherwise. Then check ownership, that the right public key is in the file, <code>PubkeyAuthentication yes</code> in <code>sshd_config</code>, and the server log (<code>journalctl -u sshd</code> or <code>/var/log/auth.log</code>) or <code>ssh -v</code> for the reason.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0104","card":"q-linux-013-ssh","sec":"linux-s014","q":"Which <code>sshd_config</code> settings do you change to harden SSH, and how do you apply them safely?","a":"<p>Set <code>PermitRootLogin no</code>, <code>PasswordAuthentication no</code> once keys work, and optionally <code>AllowUsers</code> and a changed port. Validate with <code>sshd -t</code>, keep an existing session open, reload the service, and test a new session before closing the first one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0105","card":"q-linux-013-ssh","sec":"linux-s014","q":"How do you use SSH port forwarding to reach a database that is only open on the remote host's localhost?","a":"<p>Use a local forward: <code>ssh -L 5433:localhost:5432 user@host</code> makes <code>localhost:5433</code> on your machine connect to port 5432 as seen from the remote host. <code>-R</code> does the reverse (remote port to your side) and <code>-D</code> creates a SOCKS proxy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0106","card":"q-linux-013-ssh","sec":"linux-s014","q":"Why does the SSH client warn \"REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED\", and should you simply delete the entry?","a":"<p>The server's host key differs from the one saved in <code>~/.ssh/known_hosts</code>. The reason may be a reinstall or new instance, or a man-in-the-middle attack. Verify the new fingerprint through a trusted channel first, then remove the old entry with <code>ssh-keygen -R host</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0107","card":"q-linux-013-ssh","sec":"linux-s014","q":"What does <code>ssh-keygen -t ed25519</code> offer over <code>-t rsa</code>?","a":"<p>Ed25519 keys are short, fast and considered strong, and they have fewer pitfalls with key size choice. RSA is still accepted everywhere but needs at least 2048 bits, preferably 3072 or more. Very old systems may not support Ed25519.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0108","card":"q-linux-014-hardening-selinux-and-logs","sec":"linux-s015","q":"How do you harden a Linux system?","a":"<p>Update packages, disable root SSH, use keys, set a firewall (<code>ufw</code>), enable SELinux and monitor logs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0109","card":"q-linux-014-hardening-selinux-and-logs","sec":"linux-s015","q":"What is SELinux?","a":"<p>A kernel module for mandatory access control. It restricts processes via policies (Targeted mode).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0110","card":"q-linux-014-hardening-selinux-and-logs","sec":"linux-s015","q":"How do you monitor system logs?","a":"<p>Read <code>/var/log</code> (<code>tail -f /var/log/syslog</code>) and use <code>journalctl -u sshd</code> for systemd logs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0111","card":"q-linux-014-hardening-selinux-and-logs","sec":"linux-s015","q":"What are the SELinux modes, and why is <code>setenforce 0</code> not a fix for a denial?","a":"<p>Enforcing applies and logs policy, permissive only logs, and disabled turns it off. Switching to permissive to make an application work removes the protection. Instead read the denial in <code>/var/log/audit/audit.log</code> (or <code>ausearch -m avc</code>), and fix the file context (<code>restorecon</code>, <code>semanage fcontext</code>), a boolean (<code>setsebool -P</code>) or the policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0112","card":"q-linux-014-hardening-selinux-and-logs","sec":"linux-s015","q":"What is the difference between SELinux and file permissions (DAC)?","a":"<p>Standard permissions are discretionary: the owner decides who gets access. SELinux is mandatory access control: policy labels on processes and files decide, and even root cannot override them. Both checks must allow the action. SELinux limits damage when a service is compromised.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0113","card":"q-linux-014-hardening-selinux-and-logs","sec":"linux-s015","q":"What is AppArmor and how does it differ from SELinux?","a":"<p>AppArmor is a Linux security module that confines programs with path-based profiles. It is the default on Ubuntu and SUSE. SELinux uses labels on files and processes, which is more fine-grained and more complex. Both are mandatory access control and neither is available together with the other on a normal host.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0114","card":"q-linux-014-hardening-selinux-and-logs","sec":"linux-s015","q":"What do <code>/var/log/auth.log</code> (or <code>/var/log/secure</code>) and <code>/var/log/messages</code> (or <code>syslog</code>) contain?","a":"<p>The auth log records logins, <code>sudo</code> use and SSH attempts and is on Debian-based systems <code>auth.log</code> and on the RHEL family <code>secure</code>. <code>syslog</code> or <code>messages</code> has general system messages. Which files exist depends on the distribution, and on systemd hosts the same data is also in the journal.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0115","card":"q-linux-014-hardening-selinux-and-logs","sec":"linux-s015","q":"How do you read only the errors from the last boot with the journal?","a":"<p>Run <code>journalctl -b -p err</code>. <code>-b</code> limits to the current boot (<code>-b -1</code> is the previous one), <code>-p err</code> shows priority error and worse, <code>-u unit</code> filters one service and <code>--since \"1 hour ago\"</code> limits time. The journal is only kept across reboots if persistent storage is enabled (<code>/var/log/journal</code> exists).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0116","card":"q-linux-014-hardening-selinux-and-logs","sec":"linux-s015","q":"What does <code>logrotate</code> do and why is it needed?","a":"<p>It rotates, compresses and deletes old log files by size or age according to configs in <code>/etc/logrotate.d/</code>. Without it, logs grow until they fill the disk. A <code>postrotate</code> script or <code>copytruncate</code> makes the application write to the new file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0117","card":"q-linux-014-hardening-selinux-and-logs","sec":"linux-s015","q":"What does <code>fail2ban</code> do?","a":"<p>It reads logs (such as the SSH auth log) and temporarily bans IP addresses with repeated failed logins by adding firewall rules. It reduces brute-force noise but is no replacement for key-only login.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0118","card":"q-linux-015-init-vs-systemd-and-building-from-source","sec":"linux-s016","q":"init vs. systemd?","a":"<p>init is sequential and script-based. systemd is parallel and dependency-driven, with logging and cgroups.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0119","card":"q-linux-015-init-vs-systemd-and-building-from-source","sec":"linux-s016","q":"How do you compile and install from source?","a":"<p>Extract (<code>tar -xzvf</code>), then <code>./configure</code>, <code>make</code>, <code>sudo make install</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0120","card":"q-linux-015-init-vs-systemd-and-building-from-source","sec":"linux-s016","q":"What is a systemd target and how does it relate to the old runlevels?","a":"<p>A target groups units into a system state. <code>multi-user.target</code> is roughly the old runlevel 3 (text, networking) and <code>graphical.target</code> is like 5. Check and change the default with <code>systemctl get-default</code> and <code>systemctl set-default</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0121","card":"q-linux-015-init-vs-systemd-and-building-from-source","sec":"linux-s016","q":"How do you see why a systemd service failed?","a":"<p>Run <code>systemctl status name</code> for the state and last log lines, then <code>journalctl -u name -b</code> for the full log. <code>systemctl cat name</code> shows the effective unit file and <code>systemd-analyze verify</code> can catch unit syntax errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0122","card":"q-linux-015-init-vs-systemd-and-building-from-source","sec":"linux-s016","q":"What is the difference between <code>Requires=</code> and <code>After=</code> in a systemd unit?","a":"<p><code>Requires=</code> (or the weaker <code>Wants=</code>) declares a dependency, so starting the unit also starts the other. <code>After=</code> only sets the order. They are independent, so to start a service after the network is up and also pull it in you normally need both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0123","card":"q-linux-015-init-vs-systemd-and-building-from-source","sec":"linux-s016","q":"Why use <code>checkinstall</code> or a package, rather than <code>make install</code>, when you build from source?","a":"<p><code>make install</code> copies files with no record, so the package manager cannot upgrade or remove them cleanly. Installing to <code>/usr/local</code> or <code>/opt</code> by <code>./configure --prefix=...</code>, or packaging the build, keeps it tracked. The trade-off is that you must handle security updates yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0124","card":"q-linux-015-init-vs-systemd-and-building-from-source","sec":"linux-s016","q":"What does the <code>./configure</code> step do before <code>make</code>?","a":"<p>It checks that the build tools and library headers exist, applies options such as <code>--prefix</code>, and writes the Makefile for your system. A missing development package (<code>-dev</code> or <code>-devel</code>) is the usual reason it fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0125","card":"q-linux-016-kernel-modules-udev-and-cgroups","sec":"linux-s017","q":"What is a kernel module and how do you load or unload one?","a":"<p>It extends the kernel (drivers). <code>modprobe module</code> loads, <code>modprobe -r</code> unloads, <code>lsmod</code> lists.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0126","card":"q-linux-016-kernel-modules-udev-and-cgroups","sec":"linux-s017","q":"What is udev for?","a":"<p>It manages <code>/dev</code> nodes dynamically for hardware (<code>/dev/sdb</code> for a USB drive).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0127","card":"q-linux-016-kernel-modules-udev-and-cgroups","sec":"linux-s017","q":"What are cgroups?","a":"<p>They limit and isolate resources (CPU, memory) for processes and are used by containers (<code>cgcreate</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0128","card":"q-linux-016-kernel-modules-udev-and-cgroups","sec":"linux-s017","q":"What is the difference between <code>insmod</code> and <code>modprobe</code>?","a":"<p><code>insmod</code> loads one given module file and does not resolve dependencies. <code>modprobe</code> finds the module by name, loads its dependencies first, and reads options from <code>/etc/modprobe.d/</code>. Use <code>modprobe</code> in normal work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0129","card":"q-linux-016-kernel-modules-udev-and-cgroups","sec":"linux-s017","q":"How do you load a kernel module at every boot, and how do you prevent one from loading?","a":"<p>List the module name in a file under <code>/etc/modules-load.d/</code>. To block one, add <code>blacklist name</code> (or <code>install name /bin/false</code> for a stricter block) in <code>/etc/modprobe.d/</code>. You may need to rebuild the initramfs if it is loaded early.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0130","card":"q-linux-016-kernel-modules-udev-and-cgroups","sec":"linux-s017","q":"How do you see information about a loaded module and its parameters?","a":"<p><code>lsmod</code> lists loaded modules, <code>modinfo name</code> shows description and parameters, and <code>/sys/module/name/parameters/</code> shows current values. <code>dmesg</code> shows messages the module wrote when loading.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0131","card":"q-linux-016-kernel-modules-udev-and-cgroups","sec":"linux-s017","q":"How do you make a persistent kernel setting such as <code>net.ipv4.ip_forward</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1</code> for the running kernel, which is lost at reboot. For persistence, put <code>net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1</code> in a file under <code>/etc/sysctl.d/</code> and apply with <code>sysctl --system</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0132","card":"q-linux-016-kernel-modules-udev-and-cgroups","sec":"linux-s017","q":"What is the difference between cgroups and namespaces in container isolation?","a":"<p>Cgroups limit and account for usage of (CPU, memory, I/O, process count). Namespaces limit what a process can see (PIDs, network, mounts, users, hostname). Containers combine both: namespaces give the isolated view and cgroups cap usage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0133","card":"q-linux-016-kernel-modules-udev-and-cgroups","sec":"linux-s017","q":"What are cgroup v1 and v2, and how do you tell which one a host runs?","a":"<p>In v1 each controller (cpu, memory, blkio) has its own hierarchy. In v2 there is one unified hierarchy mounted at <code>/sys/fs/cgroup</code>, with different file names (for example <code>io.max</code> instead of <code>blkio.throttle.*</code>). Run <code>stat -fc %T /sys/fs/cgroup</code>: <code>cgroup2fs</code> means v2, and <code>tmpfs</code> means v1. Recent distributions default to v2.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0134","card":"q-linux-016-kernel-modules-udev-and-cgroups","sec":"linux-s017","q":"Why does <code>udev</code> matter for a disk that gets a different device name after reboot, and what do you use instead?","a":"<p>Device names like <code>/dev/sdb</code> depend on detection order and can change. Use stable names from udev such as <code>/dev/disk/by-uuid/</code> or <code>/dev/disk/by-id/</code>, and refer to UUIDs in <code>/etc/fstab</code>. A rule in <code>/etc/udev/rules.d/</code> can also set custom names or permissions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0135","card":"q-linux-017-environment-variables-and-web-server-set","sec":"linux-s018","q":"What are environment variables and how do you set them?","a":"<p>Key-value pairs such as <code>PATH</code>. Set with <code>export VAR=value</code>. Make permanent in <code>~/.bashrc</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0136","card":"q-linux-017-environment-variables-and-web-server-set","sec":"linux-s018","q":"How do you configure Linux as a web server?","a":"<p>Install Apache (<code>apt install apache2</code>), start it (<code>systemctl start</code>), set <code>/var/www/html</code> and allow port 80 (<code>ufw</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"linux-f0137","card":"q-linux-017-environment-variables-and-web-server-set","sec":"linux-s018","q":"What is the difference between a shell variable and an environment variable?","a":"<p>A shell variable exists only in the current shell. <code>export</code> marks it as an environment variable so child processes inherit a copy. Changes in a child never flow back to the parent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0138","card":"q-linux-017-environment-variables-and-web-server-set","sec":"linux-s018","q":"Why does <code>export VAR=x</code> in a script not change your terminal's environment, and how do you load it?","a":"<p>The script runs in a child process, and its environment is lost when it ends. Use <code>source script.sh</code> (or <code>. script.sh</code>) to run it in the current shell.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0139","card":"q-linux-017-environment-variables-and-web-server-set","sec":"linux-s018","q":"Which file do you put variables in so every login sees them versus only your interactive shells?","a":"<p><code>/etc/environment</code> or <code>/etc/profile.d/*.sh</code> apply system-wide at login, <code>~/.profile</code> or <code>~/.bash_profile</code> apply to your login shells, and <code>~/.bashrc</code> applies to interactive non-login shells. Services started by systemd do not read these, so use <code>Environment=</code> or <code>EnvironmentFile=</code> in the unit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0140","card":"q-linux-017-environment-variables-and-web-server-set","sec":"linux-s018","q":"Why should you not put secrets in environment variables carelessly?","a":"<p>They are visible to the process owner and root through <code>/proc/PID/environ</code>, are inherited by child processes, and often leak into crash dumps or debug logs. A secrets manager or a protected file with tight permissions is safer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0141","card":"q-linux-017-environment-variables-and-web-server-set","sec":"linux-s018","q":"How do you check that a web server such as Apache or Nginx is working after you install it?","a":"<p>Check the service (<code>systemctl status</code>), the listening socket (<code>ss -tlnp | grep :80</code>), a local request (<code>curl -I http://localhost</code>), the firewall rule for port 80 or 443, and the error log. The checks run from the process to the socket to the network path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0142","card":"q-linux-017-environment-variables-and-web-server-set","sec":"linux-s018","q":"How do you test a web server config before reloading it, and why reload instead of restart?","a":"<p>Use <code>nginx -t</code> or <code>apachectl configtest</code> to check the syntax. <code>systemctl reload</code> re-reads the config without dropping active connections, whereas <code>restart</code> stops and starts the process. A failed test means you keep the working configuration running.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0143","card":"q-linux-018-q-docker-daemon-fails-with-cannot-open-v","sec":"linux-s019","q":"On a Linux host the Docker daemon fails with \"cannot open /var/run/docker.pid\". What does that error point to and what do you check first?","a":"<p>It usually means the pid file is unreadable, left behind, or its directory is not writable. Check <code>ls -l /var/run/docker.pid</code>, whether another <code>dockerd</code> is really running (<code>pgrep dockerd</code>), free disk space and inodes (<code>df -h /var</code>, <code>df -i</code>), and <code>journalctl -u docker -b</code> for the exact reason.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0144","card":"q-linux-018-q-docker-daemon-fails-with-cannot-open-v","sec":"linux-s019","q":"Why should you confirm that no <code>dockerd</code> process is running before you delete a stale pid file?","a":"<p>A pid file marks a running instance. If a daemon is still alive and you delete the file, a second daemon could start and fight over the same socket and storage, which risks damage to container data. Only remove the file when the process is gone, and it is then safe because the file is stale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0145","card":"q-linux-018-q-docker-daemon-fails-with-cannot-open-v","sec":"linux-s019","q":"In <code>/var/run/docker.pid</code> and similar paths, why does <code>/var/run</code> often live in memory and what does it mean for stale files?","a":"<p>On modern systems <code>/var/run</code> is a symlink to <code>/run</code>, which is a <code>tmpfs</code> cleared at each boot. So stale pid files after a reboot are unusual, and a stale file points to a crash or an unclean stop while the host stayed up. A full or read-only <code>/run</code> is another cause of the failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0146","card":"q-linux-019-q-docker-daemon-is-not-responding","sec":"linux-s020","q":"The Docker CLI hangs on Linux, and <code>systemctl status docker</code> says active. How do you tell a hung daemon from a slow one?","a":"<p>Check the socket with <code>curl --unix-socket /var/run/docker.sock http://localhost/_ping</code>, which returns <code>OK</code> if the API answers. Look at <code>journalctl -u docker</code> for repeated errors, check CPU and I/O of <code>dockerd</code>, and look for blocked tasks in <code>dmesg</code>. A daemon waiting on a stuck storage or a container hook looks hung but recovers when that finishes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0147","card":"q-linux-019-q-docker-daemon-is-not-responding","sec":"linux-s020","q":"What is the difference between <code>systemctl reload docker</code> and <code>restart docker</code>, and why does it matter for running containers?","a":"<p><code>reload</code> sends SIGHUP so the daemon re-reads parts of <code>daemon.json</code> without stopping. It keeps the containers running. <code>restart</code> stops the daemon, which stops containers unless <code>live-restore</code> is enabled in the daemon configuration. Not every setting is applied by reload, so some changes still need a restart.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0148","card":"q-linux-019-q-docker-daemon-is-not-responding","sec":"linux-s020","q":"When you must restart a hung Docker daemon, what is a safe order of steps on a production host?","a":"<p>Collect evidence first (<code>journalctl -u docker</code>, <code>docker info</code>, a goroutine dump by sending SIGUSR1 to <code>dockerd</code>), drain or shift traffic if the host is behind a load balancer, then restart and verify with <code>docker ps</code> and the health of your containers. Saving the evidence first gives a root cause, since a restart erases the state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0149","card":"q-linux-020-q-docker-service-shows-high-memory-usage","sec":"linux-s021","q":"Is the memory of the <code>dockerd</code> process or the memory of the containers the larger problem, and how do you tell which one is growing?","a":"<p>Compare <code>dockerd</code>'s RSS (<code>ps -o rss,cmd -p $(pidof dockerd)</code>) against the sum from <code>docker stats --no-stream</code>. Containers' memory is accounted to their cgroups, while the daemon's own memory grows with many containers, large log streams or leaks. If containers dominate, set limits. If the daemon grows alone, check the log driver and the version for known leaks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0150","card":"q-linux-020-q-docker-service-shows-high-memory-usage","sec":"linux-s021","q":"What does <code>docker update --memory 512m &lt;container&gt;</code> do to a running container, and what is the caveat when swap is involved?","a":"<p>It changes the container's memory limit without recreating it. If the container already has a <code>--memory-swap</code> value smaller than the new limit, the update fails, and you must set both together (<code>--memory-swap</code>). A process that is already over the new limit can be OOM-killed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0151","card":"q-linux-020-q-docker-service-shows-high-memory-usage","sec":"linux-s021","q":"Why does reading <code>free -m</code> on a Docker host not tell you which container is using the memory?","a":"<p><code>free</code> shows host-wide memory including page cache, with no split by cgroup. Use <code>docker stats</code>, or the cgroup files in <code>/sys/fs/cgroup</code>, to see each container's memory usage, which is the figure the OOM killer uses for limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0152","card":"q-linux-021-q-high-load-averages-on-the-docker-host","sec":"linux-s022","q":"On a Docker host the load average is 20 on 4 cores. Which checks tell you whether CPU, disk or memory is the cause?","a":"<p>Look at <code>top</code> for <code>us</code>/<code>sy</code> (CPU-bound) against <code>wa</code> (I/O-bound), <code>vmstat 1</code> for <code>r</code> (runnable) against <code>b</code> (blocked) and <code>si/so</code> (swapping), and <code>iostat -x 1</code> for disk <code>%util</code> and <code>await</code>. Then use <code>docker stats</code> to find the container behind it. High load with low CPU use points at I/O wait or blocked tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0153","card":"q-linux-021-q-high-load-averages-on-the-docker-host","sec":"linux-s022","q":"How do you limit a noisy container's CPU so that it cannot starve the others on a Docker host?","a":"<p>Use <code>docker run --cpus 1.5</code> for a hard cap, <code>--cpu-shares</code> for a relative weight under contention, or <code>--cpuset-cpus</code> to pin cores. Shares only matter when the CPU is busy, while <code>--cpus</code> is enforced always. Memory and process limits (<code>--pids-limit</code>) protect the host in the same way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0154","card":"q-linux-021-q-high-load-averages-on-the-docker-host","sec":"linux-s022","q":"Why can a high number of container health checks or restarts raise the load average even when the apps are idle?","a":"<p>A crash-looping container repeatedly forks, starts and tears down processes, and short health-check commands spawn processes at a steady rate. Each is brief but consumes CPU and the kernel's scheduler time. Check <code>docker ps</code> for restart counts and fix the loop or lengthen the check interval.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0155","card":"q-linux-022-q-server-is-running-out-of-inodes","sec":"linux-s023","q":"A server reports \"No space left on device\" while <code>df -h</code> shows free space. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check inodes with <code>df -i</code>. A file system can run out of inodes (many tiny files) while blocks are free, and then no new file can be created. Another cause is a process holding deleted files, which <code>lsof +L1</code> shows. Use <code>find -xdev</code> with <code>-printf '%h\\n' | sort | uniq -c</code> to find the directory holding the most files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0156","card":"q-linux-022-q-server-is-running-out-of-inodes","sec":"linux-s023","q":"Which workloads typically use up inodes on a Linux host and how do you prevent it?","a":"<p>Millions of small files such as session files, mail queues, caches and many image layers or build artifacts. Clean them with a scheduled job, rotate or expire caches, and on ext4 note that the inode count is fixed when the file system is created (<code>mkfs.ext4 -i</code> sets bytes per inode). XFS allocates inodes dynamically, so it suffers less.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0157","card":"q-linux-022-q-server-is-running-out-of-inodes","sec":"linux-s023","q":"Why is <code>rm -rf dir/*</code> sometimes failing with \"Argument list too long\" for a directory with a million files, and what do you use instead?","a":"<p>The shell expands <code>*</code> into more arguments than the kernel's limit allows for one command. Use <code>find dir -type f -delete</code>, or <code>find dir -type f -print0 | xargs -0 rm</code>, which streams the names in batches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0158","card":"q-linux-023-q-root-file-system-is-full-on-the-docker","sec":"linux-s024","q":"The root file system of a Linux host is 100% full. What is a safe order of actions to get service back?","a":"<p>Find the large items (<code>df -h</code>, <code>du -xh --max-depth=1 / | sort -h</code>), then free space where it is safe: rotated and compressed logs, package caches (<code>apt clean</code>), old kernels, and unused Docker objects. Check for deleted-but-open files (<code>lsof +L1</code>). Then add a fix that stops it from repeating, such as <code>logrotate</code>, a separate <code>/var/lib/docker</code> mount, or alerts at 80%.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0159","card":"q-linux-023-q-root-file-system-is-full-on-the-docker","sec":"linux-s024","q":"Why is <code>truncate -s 0 file.log</code> safer than <code>rm file.log</code> for a log file a running process writes to?","a":"<p>Removing the file leaves the process writing to the deleted inode, so the space is not freed until the process restarts. Truncating empties the same inode, so the space returns at once and the process keeps a valid file handle. Prefer proper log rotation over manual truncation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0160","card":"q-linux-023-q-root-file-system-is-full-on-the-docker","sec":"linux-s024","q":"Why is it better to keep Docker's data directory on its own file system rather than on the root file system?","a":"<p>Images, layers, volumes and container logs in <code>/var/lib/docker</code> grow without a natural bound. On its own volume a full Docker store cannot take the whole host down, and you can size, monitor and back it up on its own. The default location is <code>/var/lib/docker</code> and can be changed with <code>data-root</code> in <code>daemon.json</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0161","card":"q-linux-024-q-high-i-o-wait-times-on-the-docker-host","sec":"linux-s025","q":"How do you find which process or container causes heavy disk I/O on a Linux host?","a":"<p>Run <code>iostat -x 1</code> to see which device is busy (<code>%util</code>, <code>await</code>), then <code>iotop -o</code> or <code>pidstat -d 1</code> to find the processes doing the I/O. Map a process to a container through its cgroup (<code>cat /proc/PID/cgroup</code>) or with <code>docker top</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0162","card":"q-linux-024-q-high-i-o-wait-times-on-the-docker-host","sec":"linux-s025","q":"What does <code>ionice</code> do and how is it different from <code>nice</code>?","a":"<p><code>nice</code> affects CPU scheduling and <code>ionice</code> affects the disk I/O scheduling class and priority of a process, for example <code>ionice -c3</code> (idle class) lets a backup run only when the disk is idle. The classes take effect with schedulers that support them (such as BFQ), and not every scheduler honours them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0163","card":"q-linux-024-q-high-i-o-wait-times-on-the-docker-host","sec":"linux-s025","q":"Which storage choices on a Docker host reduce I/O wait for write-heavy containers?","a":"<p>Put write-heavy data on a volume (or bind mount) rather than the container's writable layer, because the overlay copy-on-write path is slower. Use fast local SSD or NVMe, keep logs from filling a slow disk, and avoid network file systems for databases. Throttling a noisy neighbour at the cgroup level also helps.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0164","card":"q-linux-025-q-how-do-you-manage-excessive-journald-l","sec":"linux-s026","q":"What is the difference between <code>journalctl --vacuum-size=500M</code> and setting <code>SystemMaxUse=500M</code> in <code>journald.conf</code>?","a":"<p><code>--vacuum-size</code> is a one-time cleanup that deletes the oldest archived journal files until the total is under the size. <code>SystemMaxUse</code> is a permanent cap that journald enforces as it writes. Use the vacuum to recover space now and the setting to prevent a repeat.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0165","card":"q-linux-025-q-how-do-you-manage-excessive-journald-l","sec":"linux-s026","q":"Which <code>journald.conf</code> settings control how long and how much the journal keeps, and what is the difference between the <code>System</code> and <code>Runtime</code> variants?","a":"<p><code>SystemMaxUse</code> limits disk use in <code>/var/log/journal</code>, <code>MaxRetentionSec</code> limits age, and <code>SystemMaxFileSize</code> limits each file. The <code>Runtime</code> variants apply to the volatile journal in <code>/run/log/journal</code>, which is used when <code>Storage=volatile</code> or when <code>/var/log/journal</code> does not exist.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0166","card":"q-linux-025-q-how-do-you-manage-excessive-journald-l","sec":"linux-s026","q":"How can a single noisy service flood the journal, and what are your options?","a":"<p>A service logging in a tight loop or at debug level fills the journal and triggers rate-limiting messages. Fix the application's log level first. In the unit you can set <code>LogRateLimitIntervalSec</code> and <code>LogRateLimitBurst</code>, or <code>journald.conf</code> <code>RateLimitIntervalSec</code> and <code>RateLimitBurst</code> (names differ in older systemd versions). Use <code>journalctl -u name --since</code> to find which unit is loudest.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0167","card":"q-linux-026-q-a-port-conflict-prevents-a-container-f","sec":"linux-s027","q":"A container fails to start with \"address already in use\" on a published port. How do you find the holder of the port?","a":"<p>Run <code>ss -tlnp | grep :80</code> or <code>lsof -i :80</code> on the host to find the process. It may be another container, a host service such as Nginx, or a leftover <code>docker-proxy</code>. Then stop that service, or publish your container on another host port such as <code>-p 8080:80</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0168","card":"q-linux-026-q-a-port-conflict-prevents-a-container-f","sec":"linux-s027","q":"Why is killing the process on the port with <code>kill -9</code> a poor first step on a production host?","a":"<p>The process may be a required service, and <code>-9</code> gives it no chance to clean up. Identify what it is, ask whether it should be running, and stop it through its manager (<code>systemctl stop</code>, <code>docker stop</code>). Choosing a different host port or fixing the deployment is often the real answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0169","card":"q-linux-026-q-a-port-conflict-prevents-a-container-f","sec":"linux-s027","q":"In <code>docker run -p 80:80</code>, which side is the host side, and how would you let two containers that both listen on 80 run on one host?","a":"<p>The left number is the host port and the right is the container port. Two containers cannot share one host port, so map them to different host ports (<code>-p 8081:80</code>, <code>-p 8082:80</code>), or put a reverse proxy in front on port 80 that routes to them by name or path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0170","card":"q-linux-027-q-a-container-fails-due-to-host-permissi","sec":"linux-s028","q":"A container fails with \"permission denied while trying to connect to the Docker daemon socket\". What is the cause and what are the trade-offs of the fix?","a":"<p>The user lacks access to <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code>, which is owned by <code>root:docker</code>. Adding the user to the <code>docker</code> group (<code>usermod -aG docker user</code>, then log in again) fixes it, but membership in that group is effectively root on the host. For tighter control use rootless Docker or <code>sudo</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0171","card":"q-linux-027-q-a-container-fails-due-to-host-permissi","sec":"linux-s028","q":"A container's process cannot write to a bind-mounted host directory. What do you check?","a":"<p>Compare the numeric UID and GID the container process runs as (<code>docker exec c id</code>) with the owner and mode of the host directory (<code>ls -ln</code>). Fix ownership on the host, or run the container with <code>--user $(id -u):$(id -g)</code>. On SELinux hosts, also check the label (<code>ls -Z</code>, <code>getenforce</code>) and the <code>:z</code> or <code>:Z</code> mount option, and on Ubuntu check AppArmor with <code>aa-status</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0172","card":"q-linux-027-q-a-container-fails-due-to-host-permissi","sec":"linux-s028","q":"Why is running a container with <code>--user</code> set to a non-root user recommended?","a":"<p>If a process escapes the container, a non-root UID limits what it can do on the host and inside mounted volumes. Many images default to root, so set <code>USER</code> in the Dockerfile or <code>--user</code> at run time. The cost is that you must fix file ownership and avoid binding ports under 1024 without extra capabilities.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0173","card":"q-linux-028-q-kernel-panic-on-the-docker-host","sec":"linux-s029","q":"A Linux server rebooted unexpectedly and you suspect a kernel panic. Where do you look for evidence?","a":"<p>Check the previous boot's log with <code>journalctl -b -1 -k</code>, then <code>/var/log/messages</code> or <code>/var/log/kern.log</code>, and <code>/var/crash</code> for a kdump vmcore if <code>kdump</code> was enabled. Also check hardware logs (<code>mcelog</code>, BMC or <code>dmesg</code>), recent kernel or driver updates, and storage or memory errors. Without a persistent journal or kdump the panic message may be lost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0174","card":"q-linux-028-q-kernel-panic-on-the-docker-host","sec":"linux-s029","q":"What does <code>kdump</code> do and why must you set it up before the panic happens?","a":"<p><code>kdump</code> reserves memory for a second small kernel (a crash kernel) that boots when the main kernel panics and writes a memory dump (<code>vmcore</code>) to disk. It must be enabled in advance, with <code>crashkernel=</code> reserved at boot, because after the panic there is nothing left to capture. Analyse the dump with the <code>crash</code> utility and matching kernel debug symbols.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0175","card":"q-linux-028-q-kernel-panic-on-the-docker-host","sec":"linux-s029","q":"What is the difference between a kernel panic and a kernel oops?","a":"<p>An oops is a kernel error that the kernel survives by killing the offending task, and the system may keep running but is unreliable. A panic is a fatal error where the kernel halts, and the system reboots if <code>kernel.panic</code> is set to a number of seconds. Setting <code>kernel.panic_on_oops=1</code> turns oopses into panics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"linux-f0176","card":"q-linux-028-q-kernel-panic-on-the-docker-host","sec":"linux-s029","q":"Why can containers on a host be the trigger for a kernel panic even though they run in user space?","a":"<p>All containers share the host kernel, so a kernel bug triggered by a container workload, a faulty storage or network driver, or a memory problem can crash the whole host. Keep the kernel and drivers updated, test upgrades, and avoid exotic drivers. This is also a reason for stronger isolation (VMs) for untrusted code.</p>","hand":true}];
